<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles MacCarthy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">killed <a href="/years/1824">1824</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sir Charles MacCarthy, MacCarthy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Charles MacCarthy was a British colonial governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> settlements in the early nineteenth century. He led a British expedition against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a> and was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nsamankow">Battle of Nsamankow</a> in 1824, when his force was annihilated. His skull was reputedly kept by the Asante as a trophy.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire's most celebrated victory came on 21 January 1824 at the Battle of Nsamankow, where an Asante army annihilated a British-led force under Governor <b>Sir Charles MacCarthy</b>, who was killed; his skull was reputedly kept in Kumasi as a trophy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Fante and the Ga of the new town of Accra looked to the British for protection, and in 1821 the Crown dissolved the company and gave the forts to <b>Charles MacCarthy</b>, governor of Sierra Leone, with a mandate to impose peace and end the slave trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1821, after the dissolution of the Company of Merchants, Britain placed its forts on the Gold Coast and its post on the Gambia under the governor of Sierra Leone, <b>Charles MacCarthy</b>, as the British West African Settlements.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-west-africa">British West Africa</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 12th century the <b>MacCarthys</b> of Desmond and then the Anglo-Normans pushed the Corcu Loigde to the coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/corcu-loigde">Corcu Loigde</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Glanmire in 1118 divided Munster, giving the south, Deasmumu, to the <b>MacCarthys</b>, heirs of the Eoganacht kings of Cashel.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-desmond">Kingdom of Desmond</a></li><li><blockquote>The area then fell within the Kingdom of Desmond under the <b>MacCarthys</b>, with the O'Donoghues as local lords around Killarney.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-iarmumu">Kingdom of Iarmumu</a></li></ul></section>
